.TH GSURF 1 "2026" "gsurf 0.5.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gsurf \- GObject Surf, a minimal modular web browser
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gsurf
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ URI ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gsurf
is a GLib/GObject web browser, a port of the suckless
.B surf
browser. Nearly all of its logic lives in the shared library
.B libgsurf
so other applications can embed it via GObject Introspection. Features that
would be surf
.I patches
are implemented as runtime-loaded
.B .so
modules (tabs, ad blocking, a vim-style modal layer, link hinting, history,
bookmarks, a find bar, tmux-style panes and windows
.RB ( wmux ,
off by default), an MCP server for AI control, and more).
.PP
Configuration is read from a YAML file and, optionally, a compiled C config.
Vim-style modal navigation is available through the
.B modal
module.
.SH GOPHER AND GEMINI
Gopher and Gemini URIs work directly, without a proxy or optional module.
Gopher menus and Gemini Gemtext become linked pages; search/input responses
display forms. Gemini redirects require following the displayed link.
Gemini uses TLS with persistent certificate pins under
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/gsurf/gemini/certificates .
Self-signed issuers are accepted, but hostname and validity checks remain;
a changed certificate is rejected until its pin is explicitly removed after
verification. Gemini client-certificate authentication is not implemented.
Gopher+ supports framed transfers, attributes, alternate views, and ASK forms;
file-transfer form fields are unsupported. ASK values appear in the resulting
URI and travel unencrypted. Requests have a 30-second deadline and a 32 MiB
body limit; Gopher+ requests including form data are limited to 64 KiB.
See
.I docs/protocols.org
for supported item types, trust behavior, and integration details.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config =\fIPATH\fR
Use the specified YAML config file instead of searching the default locations.
.TP
.BR \-\-c\-config =\fIPATH\fR
Use the specified C config file (compiled and loaded at startup).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-yaml\-config
Skip YAML config loading and use the built-in defaults.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-c\-config
Skip C config compilation and loading.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-modules
Do not load any modules.
.TP
.B \-\-generate\-yaml\-config
Print the default YAML configuration to standard output and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-generate\-c\-config
Print the default C configuration to standard output and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-modules
List the available modules in alphabetical order and exit.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fullscreen
Start in fullscreen mode.
.TP
.BR \-\-lrg [=\fIMODE\fR]
Use the libregnum/raylib backend in a raylib window (builds with
LRG_BACKEND=1).
.I MODE
is
.B 2d
(the default: the active tab, flat) or
.BR 3d [:\fIARRANGEMENT\fR][:\fIENVIRONMENT\fR],
every tab as a panel in a 3D scene: arrangement carousel, grid, stack or
free; environment studio, sky, workshop, cockpit or void, in either order
(e.g. \-\-lrg=3d:grid:sky).
.B 3dvr
is reserved. See docs/lrg-3d.org and gsurfctl(1) for the 3d commands.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version (and active backend) and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-kiosk
Require one URI, hide browser chrome, and disable URL entry, new views/popups,
native context menus, and the inspector. Enabled modules still load; chrome
widgets are not packed. Normal page navigation remains available. Fullscreen
is optional; add \-\-fullscreen if wanted. Use \-\-no-modules to skip modules.
.TP
.B \-\-pwa
Install the single HTTP(S) URI as a desktop launcher running gsurf in kiosk mode,
then exit without initializing a display. Writes to $XDG_DATA_HOME/applications
(default ~/.local/share/applications), or /usr/local/share/applications when run
as effective root. Reinstalling the same URI updates its launcher.
.TP
.BR \-\-pwa\-name =\fINAME\fR
Desktop launcher display name. Defaults to the site's hostname. Requires
\-\-pwa. Launchers use the gsurf icon and ordinary browser storage; this does
not install a web manifest, offline bundle, or separate profile.
.TP
.BR \-\-pwa\-container =\fIID\fR
Always open the installed app in this container: the launcher runs
gsurf \-\-kiosk \-\-container \fIID\fR. Without it the app is placed by the
container rules like any startup tab. Requires \-\-pwa.
.TP
.BR \-\-container =\fIID\fR
Open the startup page in this container (an id, a container name in any case,
.B temporary
or
.BR default )
instead of where the container rules would place it. An unknown container is
reported and the rules decide.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-pwa
List the installed PWA launchers, per user and system-wide: an
.I ID
(a slug of the name, e.g. "my-mail"; a short URL hash is added when two apps
share a name), the name, the pinned container (\- when the container rules
place it), the scope (user or system) and the URL. Then exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-uninstall\-pwa =\fIID\fR
Uninstall the PWA with that
.I ID
from \-\-list\-pwa (its exact name or URL works too when only one app
matches; several matches are refused with their ids). The launcher goes to
the trash where the filesystem has one, otherwise it is deleted.
System-wide launchers need root, as installing them does.
.SH ADDRESSES
The positional argument and the address bar share one normalizer.
A bare host such as
.I example.com
or
.I localhost:8080
gains an
.B https://
prefix. Absolute paths,
.IR ./ ,
.IR ../ ,
.I ~/
and
.I ~user/
paths, relative paths containing a slash that are not hosts, and the name of
a file that exists in the working directory open as
.B file://
URIs.
.BR file:///path ,
.BR file://localhost/path ,
.B file:/path
and uppercase
.B FILE:
are accepted. Spaces and non-ASCII names are percent-encoded. A query or
fragment on a file URI is kept. A file URI with a foreign host is not
rewritten onto the local disk.
.PP
Other schemes are passed through with their original spelling, including
.BR http ,
.BR https ,
.BR about ,
.BR data ,
.BR mailto ,
.BR view-source ,
.B gopher
and
.BR gemini .
Text that is neither a URI nor a path is left for the search-engine module.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
gsurf /usr/share/doc/index.html
gsurf ./page.html
gsurf ~/notes/index.html
gsurf file:///tmp/page.html#section
gsurf \-\-kiosk https://example.org
gsurf \-\-pwa \-\-pwa\-name 'My Mail' https://mail.example.org
sudo gsurf \-\-pwa https://dashboard.example.org
gsurf \-\-pwa \-\-pwa\-container personal https://mail.google.com
gsurf \-\-list\-pwa
gsurf \-\-uninstall\-pwa my\-mail
.fi
.SH KEY BINDINGS
With the default configuration, common bindings include
.BR Ctrl+l " (focus the address bar),"
.BR Ctrl+t / Ctrl+w " (new/close tab),"
.BR Ctrl+Tab " (next tab),"
.BR / " (find in page) with " n / N " (next/previous match),"
.BR f " (vim-style link hints),"
.BR ? " (live keybinding overlay), and"
.BR Ctrl+ "+/" Ctrl\- " (zoom)."
The
.B modal
module adds vim-style normal-mode keys
.RB ( h / j / k / l ", " gg / G ", " H / L ", " i ", " Esc ).
See
.B \-\-generate\-yaml\-config
for the full, documented binding set.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/gsurf/config.yaml
Per-user YAML configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gsurf/config.c
Optional per-user C configuration (compiled at startup).
.TP
.I @DATADIR@/gsurf/
System data directory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GSURF_MODULE_PATH
Override the directory that modules are loaded from.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR surf (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Zach Podbielniak.
